# Middle East batch (1461-2023): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"d-trebizond": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II set out to remove the last Greek state that could claim the Roman inheritance and to close the southern Black Sea to rivals. Trebizond surrendered after a siege in August 1461, and its emperor was executed two years later. The Byzantine world was fully absorbed, and the Black Sea became an Ottoman sea within two decades.",
 now="Trebizond's fall gave the Ottomans the whole southern shore of the Black Sea and ended the last Greek claim to the Roman inheritance. The Pontic Greek communities that stayed under Ottoman rule were uprooted in the exchanges of the 1920s.",
 story="The Empire of Trebizond, a Greek state on the Black Sea ruled by the Komnenos family since 1204, had sought an alliance against the Ottomans with Aq Qoyunlu ruler Uzun Hasan. Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II besieged the city by land and sea. Trebizond Emperor David Komnenos surrendered in August 1461. He was taken to Constantinople and executed with his sons in 1463.",
 why="Mehmed wanted every rival claimant to the Roman inheritance gone and the Black Sea trade in Ottoman hands. Trebizond's approach to Uzun Hasan made it a threat as well as a prize.",
 then="The Crimean Khanate became an Ottoman vassal in 1475 and the Black Sea an Ottoman lake. Greek Orthodox communities remained on the Pontic coast until the expulsions and the population exchange of the 1920s.",
 fact="Trebizond outlived Constantinople by eight years, the last Byzantine successor state to fall."),
"as15015": dict(
 bluf="Persian Shah Ismail I set out to turn his Sufi following into a state and to bind it to him by a faith that set it apart from every neighbour. He took Tabriz in 1501, crowned himself shah and made Twelver Shi'ism the state religion of a mostly Sunni country, by force where needed. Iran gained a religious identity distinct from the Ottomans and the Uzbeks, and the Sunni-Shi'a line he drew still organises Middle Eastern politics.",
 now="Iran remains the leading Shi'a state and backs Shi'a and allied movements across the region; the Saudi-Iranian rivalry is often framed in the Sunni-Shi'a terms fixed in 1501.",
 story="The collapse of the Turkmen states of western Iran left no settled power there. Persian Shah Ismail I, hereditary head of the Safavid Sufi order and about 14 years old, took Tabriz in 1501 at the head of Turkmen warriors called the Qizilbash and crowned himself shah. He declared Twelver Shi'a Islam the state religion and enforced it, at times violently. He brought Shi'a jurists from Lebanon and Bahrain to build a clerical establishment.",
 why="The fall of the earlier Turkmen states left a vacuum. Ismail combined charismatic religious authority over the Qizilbash with their tribal military strength. Shi'ism set the new state apart from the Sunni Ottomans and Uzbeks.",
 then="Iran became the heartland of Shi'a Islam, with a clerical class that later gained power independent of the throne. The Ottoman-Safavid wars fixed a frontier through Mesopotamia and the Caucasus.",
 fact="The Qizilbash, 'red heads', took their name from a red cap with twelve folds for the twelve Shi'a imams."),
"me-chaldiran": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Selim I marched east to stop Persian Shah Ismail I from winning Anatolia's Turkmen tribes to Shi'ism and to Safavid allegiance. His artillery and musketeers destroyed the Safavid cavalry at Chaldiran in 1514. Safavid expansion into Anatolia ended, the frontier between the Sunni Ottoman and Shi'a Safavid worlds was fixed on the ground, and gunpowder armies had beaten tribal cavalry in the open.",
 now="Turkey's border with Iran broadly follows the line fixed by the Treaty of Zuhab in 1639, which ended the wars Chaldiran began. The Sunni-Shi'a division between the two states persists.",
 story="Persian Shah Ismail I's missionaries had won followers among the Turkmen tribes of eastern Anatolia. Ottoman Sultan Selim I repressed suspected Safavid sympathisers and marched east in 1514. At Chaldiran in northwest Iran his artillery and Janissary musketeers, behind a barrier of chained wagons, broke the charges of the Safavid cavalry.",
 why="Safavid preaching threatened Ottoman control of eastern Anatolia. Selim held the firearms and Ismail the horsemen.",
 then="Selim took eastern Anatolia, then turned south and conquered the Mamluks in 1516-17. The Safavids adopted firearms and later moved their capital away from the exposed frontier.",
 fact="Ismail, whose followers had held him invincible, never again led an army in battle."),
"af15176": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Selim I turned south after Chaldiran to break the Mamluk sultanate before it could ally with the Safavids. He destroyed the Mamluk army in two battles and took Syria, Egypt and the guardianship of Mecca and Medina. Ottoman territory doubled, the sultans became the leading Sunni power, and the Arab heartlands stayed under Ottoman rule for 400 years.",
 now="Custody of the holy cities confers legitimacy to this day, as the Saudi king's title 'Custodian of the Two Holy Mosques' shows. Arab memory of the Ottoman centuries, read by some as foreign rule and by others as Islamic unity, starts here.",
 story="The Ottomans and the Mamluks had long competed for the frontier lands of southeastern Anatolia and Syria. Ottoman Sultan Selim I defeated the Mamluk army at Marj Dabiq in Syria in 1516 and at Ridaniya outside Cairo in January 1517. The last Mamluk sultan, Tuman Bay, was hanged at a Cairo gate. The Ottomans took Egypt, Syria and the guardianship of Mecca and Medina.",
 why="Selim feared a Mamluk-Safavid alliance after Chaldiran. The Mamluks had been weakened by Portuguese disruption of the Red Sea trade and were slow to adopt firearms.",
 trig="Selim marched south in 1516 against a suspected Mamluk-Safavid alliance.",
 then="The sultans took the title 'Servant of the Two Holy Sanctuaries' and later claimed the caliphate. Egypt's grain and taxes funded the empire, while Mamluk households kept real power in Egyptian politics under Ottoman governors.",
 fact="Tuman Bay was hanged at Cairo's Bab Zuwayla gate, which still stands."),
"as15208": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Suleiman I inherited a full treasury and an army built for conquest, and used both to push the empire to its limits on three fronts. In 46 years he took Belgrade, Rhodes, most of Hungary and Baghdad, besieged Vienna and gave the state a law code that outlived him by three centuries. The empire reached its territorial peak under him, and the long fronts he opened fixed the costs that later slowed it.",
 now="Suleiman's law code and administration governed the Balkans and the Arab Middle East for three centuries; the provincial boundaries, land law and religious communities he fixed are the raw material of the region's modern states.",
 story="Ottoman Sultan Suleiman I came to the throne in 1520 with the treasury and army his father's conquest of Egypt and Syria had built. He took Belgrade in 1521, Rhodes in 1522 and most of Hungary after Mohács in 1526, besieged Vienna in 1529 and captured Baghdad in 1534. His navy dominated the Mediterranean. At home his law codes earned him the title 'the Lawgiver', and his architect Mimar Sinan built the mosques that define Istanbul's skyline.",
 why="The state was built to expand: conquest rewarded soldiers and officials with land revenues. Suleiman inherited the means and the Habsburgs and Safavids supplied the rivals.",
 then="By his death in 1566 the empire faced long, costly fronts against the Habsburgs in Hungary, the Safavids in Iraq and Iran and Portugal in the Indian Ocean. Expansion slowed and power shifted from sultans to grand viziers and palace factions. His alliance with France against the Habsburgs in 1536 was an early case of interest overriding religion in European diplomacy.",
 fact="Turkey's television dramas about his reign are exported across the Muslim world."),
"me-abbas": dict(
 bluf="Persian Shah Abbas I set out to free the throne from the Qizilbash chiefs who had put it there and to recover the land lost to the Ottomans and Uzbeks. He built an army of slave-soldiers and gunners loyal to him alone, took back Tabriz and Baghdad and moved his capital to Isfahan in 1598. Safavid Iran became a centralised state able to hold its own between the Ottomans and the Mughals for another century.",
 now="Abbas rebuilt Persia as a centralised state with a slave army, a capital and a silk export monopoly, which let a Shi'a Persia survive between Sunni empires; the state he made is the ancestor of modern Iran.",
 story="Persian Shah Abbas I inherited in 1587 an empire losing land to the Ottomans and the Uzbeks and run by Qizilbash tribal chiefs. He reformed the army with musketeers and artillery and took back territory from both neighbours. In 1598 he moved his capital to Isfahan and built the Naqsh-e Jahan square and its mosques.",
 why="The Qizilbash chiefs had made and unmade shahs. Abbas needed forces loyal to him alone and a capital away from their power.",
 then="He built musketeer and artillery corps from converted Caucasian slave-soldiers, retook Tabriz and Baghdad, expelled the Portuguese from Hormuz with English help in 1622 and promoted the silk trade. His habit of confining royal princes in the palace produced weak successors.",
 fact="Abbas moved the Armenian merchants of Julfa to a new suburb of Isfahan, New Julfa, to run his silk trade with Europe."),
"me-diriyah": dict(
 bluf="Preacher Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab wanted a ruler to enforce his strict reading of Islam; Diriyah's ruler Muhammad bin Saud wanted a cause and a legitimacy to expand beyond one oasis town. Their pact of 1744 gave the Al Saud the sword and the preacher's family the pulpit. It is the founding bargain of the Saudi state, which has been destroyed and refounded twice on the same terms.",
 now="The partnership remains the foundation of the Saudi state. Oil wealth later funded the spread of Wahhabi-influenced teaching worldwide; since 2017 Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman has curbed the clergy's power.",
 story="Central Arabia in the 1740s was a patchwork of oasis towns under local rulers. Preacher Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab, expelled from his home town for calling for a return to a strict reading of Islam, found shelter in Diriyah. In 1744 he and Diriyah's ruler Muhammad bin Saud agreed that the Saud family would rule and the preacher's family would guide religion.",
 why="Each side needed the other. The ruler gained religious legitimacy and a cause to expand; the preacher gained protection and power.",
 then="The first Saudi state conquered much of Arabia, including Mecca and Medina, before Ottoman-Egyptian forces destroyed it in 1818. The alliance was revived twice, finally under Abdulaziz ibn Saud in the 20th century.",
 fact="Ibn Abd al-Wahhab's descendants, the Al ash-Sheikh, still hold senior religious posts in the kingdom."),
"me-napoleonegypt": dict(
 bluf="French General Napoleon Bonaparte invaded Egypt to cut Britain's route to India and to win glory away from Paris. He beat the Mamluks on land, lost his fleet to British Rear-Admiral Horatio Nelson within weeks and abandoned the army in 1799. Mamluk power was broken, which opened the way for Muhammad Ali's state, and the expedition began the European habit of studying the Middle East on the way to dominating it.",
 now="Modern Egyptology began with the expedition's 'Description de l'Égypte' and the Rosetta Stone. The invasion was the first large European military intervention in the Middle East since the Crusades and exposed how little the Ottomans could do to stop one.",
 story="Egypt in 1798 was an Ottoman province run by Mamluk beys. French General Napoleon Bonaparte landed in July 1798 and defeated the Mamluks at the Battle of the Pyramids. British Rear-Admiral Horatio Nelson destroyed the French fleet at the mouth of the Nile weeks later and stranded the army. The expedition's savants surveyed Egypt, and in 1799 French soldiers found the Rosetta Stone, the key to deciphering hieroglyphs.",
 why="France wanted to threaten Britain's route to India and its Mediterranean trade. Napoleon wanted a command far from the politics of Paris.",
 then="Napoleon slipped back to France in 1799 and seized power. The stranded army surrendered in 1801.",
 fact="The Rosetta Stone passed to Britain under the surrender terms of 1801 and has been in the British Museum since 1802."),
"me-muhammadali": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman officer Muhammad Ali used the vacuum the French left to make himself governor of Egypt, and then to build the Middle East's first modernising state. He destroyed the Mamluk elite, raised a conscript army and state factories and sent students to Europe. His armies nearly toppled the Ottoman sultan in the 1830s until the European powers stopped him, which showed both the reach and the limits of defensive modernisation.",
 now="His dynasty ruled Egypt until the revolution of 1952, and his model of state-led modernisation built around the army has recurred across the Middle East.",
 story="The French withdrawal of 1801 left Egypt contested between Ottoman governors and the surviving Mamluks. Muhammad Ali, commander of an Albanian unit in the Ottoman army, won the support of Cairo's notables and became governor in 1805. He massacred the old Mamluk elite in 1811, built a conscript army and factories and sent students to Europe.",
 why="The French invasion had shattered Mamluk power. A skilled soldier with the backing of Cairo's notables could fill the gap.",
 then="His armies took Syria and nearly toppled the Ottoman sultan in the 1830s, until the European powers intervened in 1840 and confined him to Egypt as hereditary governor.",
 fact="He invited the Mamluk leaders to a ceremony at the Cairo Citadel in 1811 and had them shot in a narrow passage on the way out."),
"af183018": dict(
 bluf="French King Charles X sent an army to Algiers to win military prestige for an unpopular regime, using a three-year-old diplomatic insult as the pretext. Algiers fell in July 1830 and Charles fell three weeks later. France stayed for 132 years, made Algeria legally part of France and settled it with about a million Europeans, which made its war of independence one of the bloodiest of decolonisation.",
 now="The settler colony France built after 1830 made Algerian independence a war rather than a negotiation. The 1954-62 war still shapes French-Algerian relations.",
 story="In 1827 Hussein, the Dey of Algiers, struck the French consul with a fly-whisk during a dispute over debts. French King Charles X used the incident as grounds for invasion. French troops landed in June 1830 and took Algiers in July. Conquering the interior took decades against resistance led by Emir Abd al-Qadir.",
 why="Charles X wanted a victory to shore up his regime at home. The insult of 1827 supplied the pretext.",
 then="Conquest took decades against Emir Abd al-Qadir and others, with massacres and land confiscations. Algeria was divided into French departments, and European settlers took the best land.",
 fact="Charles X was overthrown in Paris three weeks after his army took Algiers."),
"as183928": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Abdülmecid I and his reformers set out to save the empire by rebuilding its army, law and administration on European lines and promising equality to every subject. The reforms built a modern bureaucracy and won European support. They strengthened the central state and failed to stop nationalist breakaways, European interference or the debts that followed.",
 now="The Tanzimat is the template for reform from above across the Middle East, and the tension between westernisation and tradition it opened still shapes Turkey.",
 story="Muhammad Ali's Egypt had nearly toppled the sultan in 1839 and the empire needed European backing. On 3 November 1839 the 16-year-old Ottoman Sultan Abdülmecid I issued the Edict of Gülhane, drafted by Foreign Minister Mustafa Reşid Pasha, promising all subjects, Muslim and non-Muslim, security of life and property and fair taxation. Over the following decades the Tanzimat, 'reorganisation', reformed the army, courts, schools and administration along European lines.",
 why="Egypt's armies had shown that the old state could lose to a province. The reformers needed European support and a stronger state to survive.",
 then="The reforms built a modern bureaucracy and angered Muslims who lost their privileged status. They failed to stop nationalist breakaways or the empire's growing debts to Europe.",
 fact="The edict was read aloud in the Gülhane rose garden below Topkapı Palace before the foreign ambassadors."),
"af186921": dict(
 bluf="French promoter Ferdinand de Lesseps and Egypt's ruler Said Pasha wanted a canal that would shorten the route between Europe and Asia and make Egypt its toll gate. The canal opened in 1869 and did both. Building it put Egypt into debts that brought British occupation in 1882, and the waterway became a strategic prize fought over for a century.",
 now="About 12 per cent of world trade passes through the canal. Its nationalisation in 1956 set off the Suez Crisis, and the Ever Given blockage of 2021 and Houthi attacks on Red Sea shipping since 2023 show how exposed the route remains.",
 story="Egypt's ruler Said Pasha granted a concession to his friend, French promoter Ferdinand de Lesseps, whose Suez Canal Company built the 164 km waterway between 1859 and 1869, at first largely with forced Egyptian labour. The canal joined the Mediterranean and the Red Sea and cut the sea voyage between Europe and India by thousands of kilometres.",
 why="A shorter route between Europe and Asia promised large profits to investors. Said Pasha's friendship with de Lesseps supplied the concession.",
 then="Egypt's ruler Ismail Pasha sold his canal shares to Britain in 1875 and the country went bankrupt. Britain occupied Egypt in 1882 to protect the canal and its investments. The canal sped European trade with Asia and East Africa and the conquest of both.",
 fact="Egypt's ruler commissioned Verdi's opera Aida around the canal opening; it premiered in Cairo in 1871."),
"d-urabi": dict(
 bluf="Egyptian Colonel Ahmed Urabi led the army to demand a constitution and an end to European control of Egypt's finances and to Turkish-speaking monopoly of senior posts. He became war minister, and Britain and France read the movement as a threat to their loans and to the Suez Canal. Britain invaded in 1882 and stayed for over 70 years, which made Egyptian nationalism anti-British from its start.",
 now="The revolt gave Britain its reason to occupy Egypt in 1882, an occupation that lasted over 70 years and made Egyptian nationalism anti-British from its start.",
 story="Egypt's debts had handed Britain and France control of its finances, and Egyptian-born officers resented the Turkish-speaking elite that held the senior posts. In September 1881 Egyptian Colonel Ahmed Urabi led troops to Khedive Tewfik's palace to demand a constitution and limits on European influence. He became war minister in 1882.",
 why="Foreign control of the treasury and a closed officer corps gave the army and the notables a shared grievance. Khedive Tewfik had no force of his own to resist.",
 then="Britain bombarded Alexandria in July 1882 and defeated Urabi at Tel el-Kebir in September, then occupied Egypt for over 70 years.",
 fact="Urabi was exiled to Ceylon and allowed home in 1901; he is remembered as an early hero of Egyptian nationalism."),
"af188224": dict(
 bluf="Britain invaded Egypt to protect the Suez Canal and European bondholders after Egypt's bankruptcy and the Urabi revolt. It won in one battle and announced a temporary occupation that lasted until 1956. Egypt became the base of British power in the Middle East, and the occupation drew Britain deeper into Africa and helped set off the Scramble for Africa.",
 now="The occupation helped set off the Scramble for Africa and made the Suez Canal a lasting symbol of Egyptian sovereignty.",
 story="Egypt's bankruptcy had put its finances under British and French control, and nationalists led by Egyptian Colonel Ahmed Urabi took power. In July 1882 British warships bombarded Alexandria. In September British General Sir Garnet Wolseley's army defeated Urabi at Tel el-Kebir and occupied Cairo.",
 why="The canal was Britain's route to India and European investors held Egypt's debt. Urabi's movement threatened both.",
 then="Britain called the occupation temporary and stayed. Egypt was formally independent from 1922, and British troops held the canal zone until 1956.",
 fact="British Prime Minister William Gladstone, who had campaigned against imperial adventures, ordered the invasion."),
"as190845": dict(
 bluf="Officers of the Committee of Union and Progress mutinied to restore the constitution and to stop the empire losing territory to Balkan nationalists and European powers. They forced Ottoman Sultan Abdülhamid II to restore the constitution of 1876 and deposed him a year later. The constitutional regime turned into a nationalist, centralising one that took the empire into the First World War and carried out the Armenian genocide.",
 now="Mustafa Kemal came from this movement, and the Young Turks' turn to Turkish nationalism led both to the Armenian genocide and to the founding of modern Turkey.",
 story="In July 1908 officers of the Committee of Union and Progress, the 'Young Turks', mutinied in Macedonia. Ottoman Sultan Abdülhamid II restored the constitution of 1876 and elections followed. A counter-coup in April 1909 failed, and the Young Turks deposed Abdülhamid.",
 why="The officers wanted a modern state able to hold the empire together. Losses to Balkan nationalists and European powers had discredited the sultan's rule.",
 then="The CUP led the empire into the First World War on Germany's side in 1914 and lost almost all its remaining Arab lands.",
 fact="Abdülhamid was exiled to Salonica, the city where the revolt against him had begun."),
"me-persianoil": dict(
 bluf="British investor William Knox D'Arcy's drillers wanted oil before his money ran out, and the British government wanted fuel for a navy switching from coal. The strike at Masjed Soleyman in May 1908 gave both, and in 1914 the government bought control of the company. The Middle East became strategically vital to Western energy, and Persian resentment of the concession's terms led to nationalisation in 1951, a coup in 1953 and lasting distrust of Britain and the United States.",
 now="The company became BP. Iranian anger over its terms led to nationalisation in 1951 and the coup of 1953, which Iran's leaders still cite against Britain and the United States.",
 story="British investor William Knox D'Arcy had won a 60-year concession over most of Persia from the Shah in 1901. After years of failure and nearly out of money, his drillers struck oil at Masjed Soleyman in May 1908. The Anglo-Persian Oil Company was formed in 1909. In 1914 the British government bought a controlling stake to fuel the Royal Navy.",
 why="The concession of 1901 gave one company most of a country for 60 years. The Royal Navy's conversion from coal to oil gave the British state a reason to own it.",
 then="Oil made the region strategically vital, and Britain's need to protect it shaped its Middle East policy for half a century.",
 fact="D'Arcy never set foot in Persia."),
"me-libya": dict(
 bluf="Italy invaded the Ottoman provinces of Tripolitania and Cyrenaica to gain a colony on the coast opposite it while the Young Turk government was weak. It won the provinces in 1912 and spent two decades subduing Libyan resistance. Ottoman defeat invited the Balkan states to attack in 1912, and Italian rule, with concentration camps in the 1930s, left Libya a lasting grievance.",
 now="Italy's suppression of the resistance, including concentration camps in Cyrenaica in the 1930s, remains a grievance in Libyan politics and in relations with Rome.",
 story="Italy invaded the Ottoman provinces of Tripolitania and Cyrenaica in 1911 and won them by treaty in 1912. Libyan resistance continued for two decades. Ottoman officers sent to organise it included Enver and Mustafa Kemal.",
 why="Italy wanted a colonial empire across the water. The Young Turk government was weak and distracted.",
 then="Ottoman defeat encouraged the Balkan states to attack the empire in 1912. Libyan resistance under Omar Mukhtar fought on until he was captured and hanged in 1931.",
 fact="On 1 November 1911 an Italian pilot dropped grenades by hand on Ottoman troops, the first aerial bombing in history."),
"me-armenian": dict(
 bluf="At war with Russia and after heavy defeats, the Young Turk government set out to remove the Armenian population from Anatolia, which it branded a disloyal fifth column. Deportations and massacres from April 1915 killed most of the deportees; counts range from about 600,000 to 1.5 million. An ancient community in Anatolia was destroyed, the survivors became a global diaspora, and the question of what to call the killing remains a dispute between Turkey, Armenia and the states that have recognised it as genocide.",
 now="Armenia, the diaspora and more than 30 states, including the United States in 2021, call the killings genocide. Turkey's government holds that Armenians died in a wartime relocation amid famine, disease and intercommunal fighting, with no plan to destroy them, and disputes the figures; the disagreement shapes its relations with Armenia and the West.",
 story="Armenians had suffered massacres in the Ottoman Empire in 1894-96. On 24 April 1915 the government arrested Armenian leaders in Constantinople. Ottoman Interior Minister Talat Pasha's ministry then ordered the deportation of Armenians from Anatolia on marches to the Syrian desert. Massacres, starvation and disease killed most of the deportees; counts range from about 600,000 to 1.5 million dead.",
 why="The Young Turk regime read the Armenians as allies of Russia after its defeats in the Caucasus. Its leaders pursued a Turkish-Muslim Anatolia.",
 then="Survivors scattered into a diaspora in Lebanon, France and the United States, and Anatolia's ethnic map was remade. Polish lawyer Raphael Lemkin coined the word 'genocide' in 1944 partly with this case in mind.",
 fact="Talat Pasha was shot dead in Berlin in 1921 by Armenian survivor Soghomon Tehlirian, whom a German jury acquitted."),
"as191648": dict(
 bluf="Britain and France, expecting the Ottomans to lose, wanted the Arab provinces divided between them before anyone else could claim them. British diplomat Sir Mark Sykes and French diplomat François Georges-Picot drew the spheres in secret in May 1916 while Britain was promising Sharif Hussein of Mecca an Arab kingdom. The mandates that followed produced Iraq, Syria, Lebanon, Jordan and Palestine, and the agreement became the standing Arab symbol of Western betrayal.",
 now="Sykes-Picot remains the symbol of Western betrayal in the Arab world. ISIS staged the 'end of Sykes-Picot' in 2014 by bulldozing the Iraq-Syria border, though the actual borders came from later deals rather than the 1916 lines.",
 story="In May 1916 British diplomat Sir Mark Sykes and French diplomat François Georges-Picot secretly agreed how to divide the Ottoman Empire's Arab provinces: France would get Syria and Lebanon, Britain southern Mesopotamia and influence across to the Gulf, with Palestine under international administration. At the same time Britain was promising Sharif Hussein of Mecca an independent Arab kingdom in return for revolt against the Ottomans. In 1917 it promised a Jewish national home in Palestine.",
 why="Britain and France wanted to secure routes to India, oil and influence before the Ottoman collapse. Each made overlapping promises to win allies.",
 then="The Bolsheviks published the agreement in 1917 and embarrassed Britain. Through the League of Nations mandates of 1920, Britain took Iraq, Palestine and Transjordan and France took Syria and Lebanon, and Arab revolts followed.",
 fact="Tsarist Russia was the third party to the deal and was assigned eastern Anatolia; the Bolsheviks found the text in its archives and published it."),
"me-balfour": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted Jewish support for the war effort and a foothold near the Suez Canal, and Zionist leaders wanted a great power's backing. British Foreign Secretary Arthur Balfour's letter of 1917 promised a 'national home for the Jewish people' in Palestine and protection of the rights of its other communities, about 90 per cent of the population. Written into Britain's mandate, the two promises pulled against each other, and the conflict between them has run for a century.",
 now="Israelis read the declaration as the first international recognition of Zionism; Palestinians read it as the root of their dispossession. Both readings shape how each side argues its case today.",
 story="Zionist leaders, among them Chaim Weizmann, had lobbied the British government through the war. On 2 November 1917 British Foreign Secretary Arthur Balfour wrote to Lord Rothschild declaring British support for 'a national home for the Jewish people' in Palestine, provided nothing was done to prejudice the rights of its 'existing non-Jewish communities', who were about 90 per cent of the population.",
 why="Britain wanted Jewish support for the war in the United States and Russia and a strategic position near the Suez Canal. Zionist lobbying supplied the text.",
 then="Britain ruled Palestine under a League of Nations mandate that incorporated the declaration from 1920.",
 fact="The declaration's operative sentence runs to 67 words."),
"af192235": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted to end a nationwide revolt without giving up the Suez Canal, Sudan or Egypt's defence; Egyptian nationalist Saad Zaghloul's Wafd wanted full independence. Britain declared Egypt independent in 1922 and kept the four matters it cared about. The half-measure satisfied no one, and 30 years of frustrated nationalism ended in the revolution of 1952 and Nasser's Arab nationalism.",
 now="Egypt's limited independence, with Britain keeping defence and Suez, set up the grievances that led to the 1952 revolution and Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser's Arab nationalism.",
 story="A nationwide revolt in 1919, led by Egyptian nationalist Saad Zaghloul's Wafd party, made British rule costly. On 28 February 1922 Britain declared Egypt independent. Egypt got a king, Fuad I, and a parliament. Britain kept control of defence, Sudan, the Suez Canal and the protection of foreign interests.",
 why="The revolt of 1919 made direct rule too costly to hold. Britain's strategic interests made full withdrawal unacceptable to London.",
 then="Egyptians resented the half-measure for 30 years, until the Free Officers' revolution of 1952.",
 fact="Britain had exiled Zaghloul to Malta in 1919, which set off the revolt; he became prime minister in 1924."),
"as192350": dict(
 bluf="Turkish nationalist leader Mustafa Kemal set out to build a secular nation-state from the Ottoman ruins and to keep Anatolia out of Allied hands. He won the war of independence, proclaimed the republic in 1923 and abolished the sultanate and the caliphate, Islamic law and the Arabic script. Turkey became the model of top-down modernisation in the Muslim world, and the suppression of religious and Kurdish identities that came with it still shapes its politics.",
 now="Atatürk's image is still everywhere in Turkey, and its politics since the 2000s under Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan have been partly a contest over his secular legacy.",
 story="After the Ottoman defeat the Allies planned to partition Anatolia under the Treaty of Sèvres of 1920. Turkish nationalist leader Mustafa Kemal led a war of independence from 1919 to 1922 that drove out Greek, French and Italian forces. He proclaimed the Republic in October 1923, abolished the caliphate in 1924, replaced Islamic law with European-style codes, adopted the Latin alphabet in 1928 and gave women the vote in 1934.",
 why="The Ottoman collapse and the Allied partition plan came first. Kemal was convinced that only a secular, Western-style nation-state could survive.",
 then="Turkey was a one-party state until 1950. Religious expression and Kurdish identity were suppressed, and the army cast itself as guardian of secularism, staging coups in 1960, 1971 and 1980.",
 fact="Turkey switched from Arabic script to the Latin alphabet in 1928 and adopted surnames in 1934. 'Atatürk' means 'Father of the Turks'."),
"d-lausanne": dict(
 bluf="Turkey's negotiator İsmet Pasha wanted the Allies to recognise the state the nationalists had won in the field and to scrap the Treaty of Sèvres; the Allies wanted a settlement they could enforce. Lausanne gave Turkey its borders and sovereignty and endorsed a compulsory exchange of about 1.6 million Greeks and Turks. Modern Turkey's frontiers date from it, and so does the practice of unmixing peoples by treaty.",
 now="Lausanne's borders still define Turkey, and its silence on Kurdish self-rule is the root of the Kurdish question in Turkey, Iraq and Syria.",
 story="Turkish nationalists under Mustafa Kemal had won their war against Greece in 1922. Turkey's negotiator İsmet Pasha, later İsmet İnönü, faced the Allied powers at Lausanne. The treaty signed on 24 July 1923 set Turkey's borders and ended Allied claims in Anatolia. An agreement reached during the talks arranged a population exchange: about 1.2 million Orthodox Christians left Turkey and about 400,000 Muslims left Greece.",
 why="Turkey had won on the ground and the Allies had no appetite for another war. Both sides accepted the exchange as the price of a stable frontier.",
 then="The Republic of Turkey was proclaimed three months later. The exchange uprooted communities that had lived together for centuries.",
 fact="The exchange ran by religion, so Turkish-speaking Christians of Anatolia went to Greece and Greek-speaking Muslims of Crete went to Turkey."),
"d-caliphate": dict(
 bluf="Mustafa Kemal wanted no rival centre of authority in the new republic, and the caliphate was the last one. Turkey's Grand National Assembly abolished it on 3 March 1924 and exiled the Ottoman family. An office that had claimed leadership of the Muslim world since the 7th century ended, and the vacuum it left has been claimed by Islamist movements ever since.",
 now="Islamist movements, including ISIS in 2014, have claimed to restore the caliphate; its absence is part of the argument they make.",
 story="The sultanate had been abolished in 1922 and the caliphate left to the last caliph, Abdulmejid II, as a religious office. On 3 March 1924 Turkey's Grand National Assembly abolished the caliphate and exiled the Ottoman family. Religious courts and schools were closed. Over the following years Turkey adopted European law codes, the Latin alphabet and votes for women.",
 why="Mustafa Kemal saw the caliphate as a rival centre of power and an obstacle to a secular state.",
 then="Attempts to revive the caliphate elsewhere came to nothing. Turkey's secularisation went further, banning religious dress for officials and replacing the Arabic script.",
 fact="Abdulmejid II, a painter, died in exile in Paris in 1944 and was buried in Medina."),
"me-saudi": dict(
 bluf="Abdulaziz ibn Saud set out from exile to recover his family's lands and, with Wahhabi backing, to take the whole peninsula. He captured Riyadh in 1902, the holy cities in 1924-25, and proclaimed the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia in 1932. A dynasty allied with clerics now held Islam's holiest sites, and oil six years later made it a key partner of the United States.",
 now="His sons have ruled the kingdom ever since; the royal family's alliance with Wahhabi clerics and with US security guarantees still defines the Saudi state.",
 story="The second Saudi state had collapsed in 1891 and the family lived in exile in Kuwait. Abdulaziz ibn Saud retook Riyadh in 1902 and conquered central Arabia over the next two decades. In 1924-25 he took the holy cities of Mecca and Medina from the Hashemites. On 23 September 1932 he proclaimed the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia.",
 why="Military skill and Wahhabi religious backing came first. British subsidies helped at times.",
 then="American geologists struck oil in 1938, and the Saudi state's partnership with the United States began.",
 fact="Ibn Saud retook Riyadh in 1902 with a few dozen men in a night raid on its fort."),
"me-saudioil": dict(
 bluf="Saudi King Abdulaziz ibn Saud needed money after the Depression cut pilgrimage income, and Standard Oil of California wanted a concession on ground no one else had tested. The gamble paid in March 1938 when Dammam No. 7 flowed in commercial quantities on the edge of the world's largest reserves. The kingdom became central to the world economy, and the US-Saudi exchange of oil for security has shaped Middle East politics since.",
 now="Saudi Aramco is one of the world's most valuable companies, and Saudi decisions on output still move world oil prices and shape relations with Washington.",
 story="Saudi King Abdulaziz ibn Saud granted a concession in 1933 to the American company Standard Oil of California. Its geologists drilled six dry or marginal wells at Dammam. Well No. 7 began producing in commercial quantities in March 1938, on the edge of what proved to be the world's largest oil reserves.",
 why="Ibn Saud needed revenue and distrusted the British. The American company offered cash and no imperial baggage.",
 then="The partnership became Aramco. The United States and Saudi Arabia formed a lasting oil-for-security relationship.",
 fact="US President Franklin Roosevelt met Ibn Saud aboard the cruiser USS Quincy in the Suez Canal in February 1945."),
"w2-alamein": dict(
 bluf="German Field Marshal Erwin Rommel drove into Egypt to take the Suez Canal and the oil beyond it; British General Bernard Montgomery's Eighth Army fought to stop him 100 km from Alexandria. Montgomery attacked on 23 October 1942 with more than twice Rommel's tanks and guns and broke the Axis line in twelve days. Egypt, the canal and Middle Eastern oil stayed Allied, and with the landings in Morocco and Algeria days later the Axis was driven out of Africa.",
 now="The victory ended the Axis threat to the Suez Canal and Middle Eastern oil, which became central to Western strategy in the region after the war.",
 story="In summer 1942 German Field Marshal Erwin Rommel's German and Italian army drove deep into Egypt and was stopped at El Alamein, about 100 km from Alexandria. On 23 October British General Bernard Montgomery's Eighth Army attacked with more than twice as many tanks and guns and with intelligence from decoded German messages. After about twelve days the Axis lines broke and Rommel retreated across Libya.",
 why="Control of Egypt, the canal and the oil beyond was the prize. Montgomery had the numbers, the supplies and the decrypts.",
 then="US and British troops landed in Morocco and Algeria days later. The Axis forces in North Africa surrendered in Tunisia in May 1943.",
 fact="Britain's church bells, silent since 1940 as an invasion alarm, were rung to mark the victory."),
"as194858": dict(
 bluf="Zionist leaders under David Ben-Gurion set out to declare a Jewish state the moment the British mandate ended; the Arab states and Palestinian leaders set out to prevent it. Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948, beat the invading armies and held more land than the UN plan had allotted, while about 700,000 Palestinians fled or were expelled. Israelis mark the date as independence and Palestinians as the Nakba, and the questions it opened remain unsettled.",
 now="Borders, refugees, Jerusalem and Palestinian statehood remain unresolved and sit at the heart of Middle East politics.",
 story="In November 1947 the UN voted to partition British-ruled Palestine into Jewish and Arab states; Jewish leaders accepted the plan, Arab leaders rejected it, and civil war broke out. On 14 May 1948, as the British mandate ended, Jewish Agency chairman David Ben-Gurion declared the State of Israel, and Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon invaded. By 1949 Israel held more territory than the UN plan had allotted, Jordan held the West Bank and Egypt held Gaza.",
 why="Decades of Zionist immigration and state-building under the mandate came first. The Holocaust's effect on Jewish refugees and world opinion followed. Arab leaders opposed a Jewish state in a land with an Arab majority.",
 trig="The end of the British mandate on 14 May 1948, after the UN partition vote.",
 then="About 700,000 Palestinians, by the UN's count of 1949, fled or were expelled and were barred from return. Over the following years hundreds of thousands of Jews left or were driven out of Arab countries, many to Israel. No peace treaties followed, and wars recurred.",
 fact="The United States recognised Israel 11 minutes after it declared independence."),
"me-egypt1952": dict(
 bluf="The Free Officers under Gamal Abdel Nasser and Muhammad Naguib set out to end a monarchy they held responsible for corruption, British influence and the defeat of 1948. They seized power on 23 July 1952 and sent King Farouk into exile. Nasser became the voice of Arab nationalism, and Egypt has been ruled by military men for almost all of the years since.",
 now="Egypt has been led by military men for almost all the time since 1952, apart from 2012-13.",
 story="Egypt's monarchy had lost the war of 1948 and its standing at home. On 23 July 1952 the Free Officers, led by Egyptian Colonel Gamal Abdel Nasser with General Muhammad Naguib as figurehead, seized power and forced King Farouk into exile. Nasser emerged as leader in 1954, launched land reform and negotiated the British out of the Suez Canal zone.",
 why="Anger at a corrupt monarchy and at British influence came first. The defeat of 1948 turned the officers against the regime.",
 then="Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal in 1956 and became the leader of Arab nationalism.",
 fact="Farouk sailed from Alexandria on the royal yacht with a 21-gun salute, by the officers' arrangement."),
"me-iran1953": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted its nationalised oil company back and the United States feared Iran drifting toward the Soviet Union; both set out to remove Iranian Prime Minister Mohammad Mosaddegh. The CIA and MI6 organised a coup with royalist officers in August 1953 that restored the Shah's power. Iran got 25 more years of autocracy, and Iranians got a grievance against the United States that the revolution of 1979 drew on.",
 now="The CIA formally acknowledged its role in 2013. Resentment of the coup helped fuel the 1979 revolution and still frames Iran's dealings with Washington.",
 story="Iranian Prime Minister Mohammad Mosaddegh nationalised the Anglo-Iranian Oil Company in 1951. Britain imposed a boycott on Iranian oil. In August 1953 the CIA and MI6 organised a coup with royalist officers that removed Mosaddegh and restored the power of Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi.",
 why="Britain wanted its oil back. The United States feared Iran would drift toward the Soviet Union.",
 then="The Shah ruled with growing autocracy for 25 years, backed by the United States and his secret police, SAVAK.",
 fact="The Shah fled to Rome when the first attempt failed and returned days later when the second succeeded."),
"af195439": dict(
 bluf="The FLN fought to end 132 years of French rule and settler domination; France fought to keep a territory it called part of France. Eight years of war with 400,000 French troops, torture and mass detention ended in independence in 1962. The war toppled the Fourth Republic, brought Charles de Gaulle back to power and left a legacy of trauma and migration that still shapes French and Algerian politics.",
 now="The war still shapes French politics, immigration debates and French-Algerian relations.",
 story="A century of settler rule had confiscated land and denied Algerian Muslims equal rights. The FLN began an armed uprising on 1 November 1954. France sent 400,000 troops, used torture and mass detention and fought the FLN inside Algiers itself.",
 why="Settler domination and the denial of equal rights came first. The example of other independence movements and France's defeat in Indochina followed.",
 trig="Coordinated FLN attacks across Algeria on 1 November 1954.",
 then="Hundreds of thousands of Algerians died by French counts; the Algerian state puts the dead at 1.5 million. The crisis brought de Gaulle to power in 1958, and he negotiated independence in the Évian Accords of 1962. About a million European settlers, and many Algerians who had fought for France, fled to France.",
 fact="France called the fighting 'events' and recognised it as a war in law only in 1999."),
"af195640": dict(
 bluf="Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal to pay for the Aswan Dam; Britain, France and Israel invaded to take it back and to bring him down. The United States forced them out within days by threatening the pound. Britain and France lost the capacity to act as independent imperial powers, and the United States and the Soviet Union stood confirmed as the arbiters of the Middle East.",
 now="The crisis marked the moment the United States replaced Britain and France as the dominant Western power in the Middle East.",
 story="In July 1956 Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalised the British- and French-owned Suez Canal Company. Britain, France and Israel secretly agreed that Israel would invade Sinai and the two European powers would intervene as 'peacekeepers' to seize the canal. When they attacked in October and November, US President Dwight Eisenhower threatened to sink the British pound, the Soviets threatened rocket attacks, and the invaders withdrew.",
 why="Nasser nationalised the canal to pay for the Aswan Dam after the United States and Britain withdrew funding. British Prime Minister Anthony Eden read him as a dictator to be removed.",
 trig="Nasser's nationalisation of the canal in July 1956.",
 then="Nasser became the hero of the Arab world, Eden resigned, and the UN sent its first armed peacekeeping force.",
 fact="Eisenhower forced Britain to retreat by threatening to dump US holdings of British pounds."),
"d-algiers": dict(
 bluf="The FLN brought the war into Algiers to win world attention; France handed French General Jacques Massu's paratroopers police powers to crush it. The paratroopers destroyed the FLN network in the city by October 1957 using systematic torture. France won the battle and lost the argument, and Algiers became the standard case of counterinsurgency's moral costs.",
 now="France won the battle with torture and lost the argument: the methods discredited the war at home and abroad, and Algeria was independent five years later.",
 story="In 1956-57 the FLN bombed cafés and called a general strike in Algiers. From January 1957 French General Jacques Massu's 10th Parachute Division took control of the city and arrested thousands; torture and disappearances were widespread. By October the FLN network in the city had been destroyed.",
 why="The FLN wanted the war in the capital where the world press could see it. The French government wanted it crushed and gave the army the powers to do so.",
 then="Revelations of torture turned French and world opinion against the war, and Algeria won independence in 1962.",
 fact="The 1966 film of the battle is still studied by armies; the Pentagon screened it in 2003 before the occupation of Iraq."),
"d-evian": dict(
 bluf="French President Charles de Gaulle had concluded that Algeria could be held only at an unacceptable cost; the FLN's provisional government wanted independence recognised. The accords of March 1962 ended the war and led to independence on 5 July. Nearly a million settlers left for France, Algerians who had served France faced reprisals, and the memory still shapes relations between the two states.",
 now="The memory of the war still shapes relations between France and Algeria, French debates on immigration and the legitimacy of Algeria's ruling elite.",
 story="French President Charles de Gaulle's government and the FLN's provisional government signed the accords at Évian-les-Bains on 18 March 1962. Algerians voted overwhelmingly for independence in a referendum on 1 July, and Algeria became independent on 5 July. Nearly a million European settlers left for France.",
 why="De Gaulle had concluded that Algeria could not be held at an acceptable cost. The FLN had the population and the world's sympathy.",
 then="The settler terror group OAS fought on. Algerians who had served France, the harkis, faced deadly reprisals.",
 fact="Algeria chose 5 July for independence, the date French troops had taken Algiers in 1830."),
"me-plo": dict(
 bluf="The Arab League created the PLO in 1964 to give Palestinians a voice it could control; after 1967 Yasser Arafat's Fatah took it over and made it an independent national movement built on armed struggle. The PLO moved from guerrilla war to recognising Israel in 1993 and gained self-rule in parts of the West Bank and Gaza. It has yet to achieve a state, and its Fatah faction now governs the West Bank's Palestinian Authority.",
 now="The PLO recognised Israel in 1993; its Fatah faction leads the Palestinian Authority in the West Bank.",
 story="Palestinians scattered across refugee camps and neighbouring states after 1948 had no political organisation of their own. The Arab League set up the Palestine Liberation Organisation in 1964. After the 1967 war, guerrilla groups led by Fatah chairman Yasser Arafat took it over and made it an independent Palestinian movement using armed attacks against Israel.",
 why="Palestinians lacked a political voice. Arab states wanted to control their cause.",
 then="After a civil war with Jordan in 1970-71, 'Black September', the PLO moved its base to Lebanon, where its presence helped trigger the civil war of 1975.",
 fact="Arafat addressed the UN General Assembly in 1974 carrying, in his words, an olive branch and a freedom fighter's gun."),
"as196764": dict(
 bluf="Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser mobilised in Sinai and closed the Straits of Tiran to force Israel back; Israeli Prime Minister Levi Eshkol's government struck first to break the encirclement. In six days Israel took Sinai, Gaza, the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights, tripling the land it controlled. The occupation of Palestinian territories, settlements and 'land for peace' became the central questions of the conflict.",
 now="Israel still holds the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights, and settlements are at the heart of today's disputes.",
 story="In May 1967 Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser expelled UN peacekeepers from Sinai, mobilised his army there and closed the Straits of Tiran to Israeli shipping. On 5 June Israel struck first and destroyed most of Egypt's air force on the ground. In six days it took Sinai and Gaza from Egypt, the West Bank and East Jerusalem from Jordan and the Golan Heights from Syria.",
 why="Tension with Syria and Soviet reports of Israeli troop movements led Nasser to mobilise. The closure of the Straits was the step Israel had long said it would treat as war.",
 trig="Egypt's closure of the Straits of Tiran in May 1967.",
 then="UN Resolution 242 set out the formula of 'land for peace'. Israel began building settlements, and Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza came under Israeli military rule. Secular Arab nationalism was discredited and Islamist movements gained ground.",
 fact="The war ran from 5 to 10 June; most of Egypt's air force was destroyed in the first three hours."),
"as197365": dict(
 bluf="Egyptian President Anwar Sadat and Syrian President Hafez al-Assad attacked on 6 October 1973 to regain the land lost in 1967 and to force negotiations; Sadat aimed for limited gains rather than victory. Early Arab advances broke Israel's sense of invincibility before Israel, resupplied by a US airlift, counterattacked across the canal. Egyptian pride was restored, the Arab oil embargo followed, and the path to a separate Egyptian-Israeli peace opened.",
 now="Israel's intelligence failure of 1973 is still debated and was compared to the failure of 7 October 2023.",
 story="On 6 October 1973, Yom Kippur, Egyptian troops crossed the Suez Canal and Syrian forces attacked on the Golan Heights. Israeli Prime Minister Golda Meir's government had read the mobilisation as a bluff. After heavy early losses, Israel, resupplied by a US airlift, counterattacked and crossed the canal before a ceasefire.",
 why="Egypt and Syria wanted their land back and an end to the diplomatic stalemate. Sadat planned limited gains that would force talks.",
 then="US diplomacy led to the Camp David Accords of 1978 and an Egypt-Israel peace treaty in 1979.",
 fact="The war fell in Ramadan as well as on Yom Kippur; Egypt calls it the October War or the Ramadan War."),
"as197365b": dict(
 bluf="Arab oil producers led by Saudi Arabia cut output and embargoed shipments to punish the United States and others for backing Israel in the October war, and to assert control over their own oil. With OPEC raising prices, oil went from about 3 to about 12 dollars a barrel by early 1974. Inflation and recession hit the West, wealth shifted to the producers, and energy security became a core strategic concern.",
 now="The embargo showed how far the world depended on Middle Eastern oil and drove energy-saving rules such as fuel-economy standards.",
 story="Days after the October war began, Arab oil producers led by Saudi King Faisal cut production and embargoed shipments to the United States and other backers of Israel. OPEC raised prices at the same time. Oil went from about 3 dollars a barrel to about 12 by early 1974.",
 why="The producers wanted to pressure Israel's backers. They also wanted to assert control over their own oil after decades of company pricing.",
 then="Queues at petrol stations, inflation and recession hit the West. Oil wealth transformed the Gulf states.",
 fact="The United States imposed a national 55 mph speed limit in 1974 to save fuel."),
"me-campdavid": dict(
 bluf="Egyptian President Anwar Sadat wanted Sinai back and US aid; Israeli Prime Minister Menachem Begin wanted Egypt out of the Arab war coalition; US President Jimmy Carter wanted a peace to his name. Thirteen days at Camp David in 1978 produced a framework, and the treaty of 1979 returned Sinai for recognition and peace. The first Arab-Israeli peace has held for over 40 years, left the Palestinian question open and cost Egypt a decade of isolation and Sadat his life.",
 now="The peace has held for over 40 years, and Egypt receives large US aid under it.",
 story="After 1973 Egyptian President Anwar Sadat flew to Jerusalem in 1977 to offer peace. US President Jimmy Carter hosted Sadat and Israeli Prime Minister Menachem Begin at Camp David for 13 days in September 1978. They agreed a framework for peace. The 1979 treaty returned Sinai to Egypt in exchange for recognition and peace.",
 why="Sadat wanted Sinai back, US aid and an end to costly wars. Israel wanted its most powerful Arab enemy out of the conflict.",
 then="Egypt was suspended from the Arab League. Sadat was assassinated by Islamist officers in 1981.",
 fact="Sadat and Begin shared the 1978 Nobel Peace Prize."),
"as197968": dict(
 bluf="Iran's clergy, bazaar and left set out to end the Shah's autocracy, and Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini set out to replace it with clerical rule. Months of strikes and protests forced Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi to flee in January 1979, and Khomeini returned to found an Islamic Republic. Political Islam became a force across the region, and Iran became a standing rival of the United States, Israel and Saudi Arabia.",
 now="Iran's clerical regime and its rivalry with the United States, Israel and Saudi Arabia still shape the region; it faced the 'Woman, Life, Freedom' protests of 2022 and fought a direct war with Israel in 2025.",
 story="Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi ruled through SAVAK and an oil boom that enriched few. Months of strikes and protests through 1978 forced him to flee in January 1979. Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini returned from exile in February and established an Islamic Republic led by clerics.",
 why="Anger at the Shah's autocracy, secret police and the inequality of the oil boom came first. Resentment of the 1953 coup and the pace of westernisation followed. The Shi'a clergy had an organised network independent of the state.",
 trig="The killing of protesters through 1978, including 'Black Friday' in September, and the Shah's departure in January 1979.",
 then="Revolutionary students seized the US embassy in November 1979, and Iraq invaded in 1980. The Islamic Republic suppressed its leftist and liberal allies and exported its revolution, notably by founding Hezbollah in Lebanon.",
 fact="Khomeini's sermons spread through Iran on smuggled cassette tapes while he was in exile in France."),
"na197984": dict(
 bluf="Iranian revolutionary students seized the US embassy to protest the Shah's admission to the United States and to forestall a repeat of 1953. They held 52 Americans for 444 days, and a rescue attempt failed in the desert. The crisis helped end US President Jimmy Carter's presidency and fixed a US-Iranian hostility that has run through sanctions, proxy wars and the nuclear dispute since.",
 now="US-Iran hostility has lasted since 1979, through sanctions, proxy wars, the nuclear dispute and, in 2025, US strikes on Iran's nuclear sites.",
 story="In October 1979 the United States admitted the deposed Shah for cancer treatment. On 4 November Iranian students stormed the US embassy in Tehran and took its staff hostage. US President Jimmy Carter's rescue mission in April 1980 failed in the desert. The 52 remaining hostages were held for 444 days.",
 why="The students read the Shah's admission as a prelude to another 1953. Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini used the seizure to sideline his moderate rivals.",
 then="The crisis helped sink Carter's presidency. The hostages were freed on 20 January 1981, minutes after US President Ronald Reagan was sworn in.",
 fact="Six American diplomats who escaped the embassy were hidden by Canada's ambassador and flown out on false passports in January 1980."),
"as198069": dict(
 bluf="Iraqi President Saddam Hussein invaded Iran to seize the Shatt al-Arab and oil-rich Khuzestan from a state in revolutionary chaos, and to stop the revolution spreading to Iraq's Shi'a majority. Eight years of trench warfare, human-wave attacks and Iraqi chemical weapons ended in 1988 at the starting line with about half a million dead. Iran's regime was entrenched by the war, and Iraq's debts to Kuwait and Saudi Arabia led Saddam to invade Kuwait in 1990.",
 now="The war shaped Iran's revolutionary identity and its security state, and Iraq's debts from it led to the invasion of Kuwait in 1990.",
 story="In September 1980 Iraqi President Saddam Hussein invaded Iran. The war became eight years of trench warfare, human-wave attacks and Iraqi chemical weapons. It ended in 1988 in stalemate on the prewar border.",
 why="Saddam wanted the Shatt al-Arab waterway and Khuzestan's oil. He feared Iran's revolution would reach Iraq's Shi'a majority.",
 then="About half a million people died by most counts, some running higher. Iraq emerged with heavy debts to Kuwait and Saudi Arabia.",
 fact="Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini said accepting the ceasefire was worse than drinking poison."),
"me-lebanon1982": dict(
 bluf="Israel invaded Lebanon to end PLO attacks from the south and to install a friendly government in Beirut. It drove the PLO out to Tunis, and its Lebanese Christian allies massacred hundreds of Palestinians at Sabra and Shatila while Israeli forces held the perimeter. Israel occupied south Lebanon for 18 years, and Iran helped build Hezbollah there, which became Lebanon's dominant armed force and Iran's closest ally.",
 now="Hezbollah became the most powerful armed force in Lebanon until Israel's 2024 offensive killed its leader Hassan Nasrallah and much of its command; in 2025 Lebanon's government moved to disarm it.",
 story="Israel invaded in June 1982 and besieged Beirut until the PLO agreed to leave for Tunisia. In September Lebanese Christian militiamen allied with Israel killed hundreds of Palestinian civilians in the Sabra and Shatila camps. An Israeli inquiry in 1983 found Israeli Defence Minister Ariel Sharon indirectly responsible, and he resigned the post.",
 why="Israel wanted an end to PLO attacks from southern Lebanon and a friendly government in Beirut.",
 trig="The shooting of Israel's ambassador in London on 3 June 1982 by the Abu Nidal group.",
 then="Iran helped create Hezbollah among Lebanon's Shi'a. Israel occupied south Lebanon until 2000.",
 fact="The commission that judged Sharon was Israel's own, and its finding ended his tenure as defence minister."),
"me-intifada": dict(
 bluf="Palestinians in the West Bank and Gaza rose against 20 years of occupation with strikes, stones and boycotts, organised locally rather than by the PLO abroad. The uprising ran for six years and killed over 1,000 Palestinians and over 150 Israelis. It won Palestinians international attention, produced Hamas, and pushed Israel and the PLO toward the Oslo talks.",
 now="The intifada pushed both sides toward the Oslo talks. Its largely unarmed mass protest contrasts with the far deadlier armed Second Intifada of 2000-05.",
 story="On 8 December 1987 an Israeli truck killed four Palestinians in Gaza. Protests spread across the West Bank and Gaza and ran until 1993. Over 1,000 Palestinians and over 150 Israelis were killed by Israeli rights-group counts.",
 why="Twenty years of occupation, settlement and economic hardship came first. Palestinians also felt the PLO abroad and the Arab states had failed them.",
 then="Hamas was founded in Gaza at the start of the uprising. In 1988 the PLO moved toward accepting a two-state framework.",
 fact="'Intifada' means 'shaking off' in Arabic."),
"as199171": dict(
 bluf="Iraqi President Saddam Hussein took Kuwait for its oil and to cancel his debts; US President George H. W. Bush built a 35-nation coalition to reverse the conquest and protect Saudi Arabia. The coalition drove Iraq out in 100 hours of ground fighting in February 1991. American military dominance after the Cold War was on display, and the US troops left in Saudi Arabia became one of Osama bin Laden's stated grievances.",
 now="The war opened an era of US military dominance in the Gulf, and the unfinished business with Saddam led to the invasion of 2003.",
 story="Iraqi President Saddam Hussein invaded Kuwait in August 1990. US President George H. W. Bush built a 35-nation coalition under a UN mandate. After five weeks of air attack, the coalition drove Iraq out of Kuwait in 100 hours of ground fighting in February 1991.",
 why="Saddam wanted Kuwait's oil and an end to the debts of the Iran-Iraq war. The coalition feared he would move on Saudi Arabia next.",
 then="US troops stayed in Saudi Arabia, which angered Osama bin Laden. Saddam survived, crushed Shi'a and Kurdish uprisings and faced sanctions and no-fly zones.",
 fact="Retreating Iraqi troops set about 600 Kuwaiti oil wells on fire."),
"me-oslo": dict(
 bluf="Israeli Prime Minister Yitzhak Rabin and PLO Chairman Yasser Arafat set out to trade recognition for self-rule and to defer the hardest questions to later talks. The accords of 1993 brought mutual recognition and a Palestinian Authority in parts of the West Bank and Gaza. The hardest issues stayed open, attacks and settlement growth continued, and the process collapsed in 2000.",
 now="The Palestinian Authority the accords created still governs parts of the West Bank, and a final peace was never reached.",
 story="After secret talks in Norway, Israeli Prime Minister Yitzhak Rabin and PLO Chairman Yasser Arafat shook hands on the White House lawn before US President Bill Clinton in September 1993. Israel recognised the PLO and the PLO recognised Israel. A Palestinian Authority was to govern parts of the West Bank and Gaza while Jerusalem, borders, refugees and settlements were negotiated.",
 why="The intifada, the end of the Cold War and the Gulf War pushed both sides to talk. Each read the other as weakened enough to deal.",
 then="Rabin was assassinated by an Israeli extremist in 1995. Final-status talks failed in 2000 and the second intifada followed. Israelis blame Palestinian violence for the collapse; Palestinians blame continued settlement growth.",
 fact="Rabin, Arafat and Israeli Foreign Minister Shimon Peres shared the 1994 Nobel Peace Prize."),
"as200374": dict(
 bluf="US President George W. Bush's administration invaded Iraq to remove Saddam Hussein, saying he held weapons of mass destruction and had ties to terrorists. Baghdad fell in three weeks and no such weapons were found. The occupation's failures produced an insurgency, a sectarian war and ISIS, enlarged Iran's influence in Iraq and damaged US credibility worldwide.",
 now="The invasion damaged trust in US intelligence and leadership, and greatly increased Iran's influence in Iraq.",
 story="The US and its allies invaded Iraq in March 2003, saying Iraqi President Saddam Hussein had weapons of mass destruction and links to terrorists. Baghdad fell within weeks. No such weapons were found. An insurgency and a sectarian civil war followed.",
 why="The Bush administration claimed Iraq held banned weapons and terrorist ties. After 9/11 it wanted Saddam removed.",
 then="Well over 100,000 Iraqi civilians died in the years of violence that followed, by documented counts. ISIS later grew out of the chaos.",
 fact="Saddam was found in December 2003 hiding in a hole near Tikrit and was hanged in 2006."),
"as201175": dict(
 bluf="Protesters across the Arab world set out to end regimes they held responsible for corruption, repression and unemployment. They toppled rulers in Tunisia, Egypt, Libya and Yemen within a year. Apart from Tunisia, for a decade, the uprisings ended in restored authoritarianism or civil war, and the grievances behind them remain.",
 now="Tunisia's democracy backslid after 2021; the grievances behind the protests remain across the region.",
 story="After Tunisian street vendor Mohamed Bouazizi set himself on fire in December 2010, protests forced Tunisian President Zine El Abidine Ben Ali out in January 2011. Egyptian President Hosni Mubarak fell in February. Libyan leader Muammar Gaddafi was overthrown with NATO air support and killed in October.",
 why="Corruption, repression, youth unemployment and food prices came first. Satellite television and social media spread the protests.",
 trig="The self-immolation of Tunisian street vendor Mohamed Bouazizi in December 2010.",
 then="Only Tunisia became a democracy. Egypt returned to military rule, and Libya, Syria and Yemen fell into civil wars that drew in outside powers, produced refugee flows to Europe and let ISIS rise.",
 fact="Tunisia's National Dialogue Quartet won the 2015 Nobel Peace Prize."),
"as201175b": dict(
 bluf="Syrian President Bashar al-Assad chose force over reform when protests spread in March 2011, and the opposition took up arms. The war killed about half a million people, displaced half the population and drew in Russia, Iran, Turkey, the United States and jihadist groups including ISIS. Assad fell to a rebel offensive in December 2024.",
 now="Assad's regime fell to a rebel offensive led by the Islamist group HTS in December 2024, and its leader Ahmed al-Sharaa now heads Syria's government.",
 story="Peaceful protests in March 2011 were met with gunfire. Armed rebellion followed, and the war drew in Iran, Russia, Turkey, the United States and jihadist groups including ISIS. About half of Syria's population was displaced.",
 why="Syrian President Bashar al-Assad's regime chose force over reform. Sectarian divisions and foreign backers turned the conflict into a long war.",
 trig="The arrest and torture of boys in Deraa for anti-regime graffiti in March 2011.",
 then="Millions of refugees fled to Turkey, Lebanon, Jordan and Europe. Russia, Iran, Hezbollah, Turkey and the United States all sent forces.",
 fact="Assad fled to Moscow as the rebels entered Damascus in December 2024."),
"d-libya2011": dict(
 bluf="Libyan rebels set out to end Muammar Gaddafi's 42-year rule, and NATO intervened under a UN mandate to protect civilians from his crackdown. Air strikes helped the rebels take Tripoli in August 2011, and Gaddafi was captured and killed in October. Libya fragmented into rival governments and militias, and Russia and China turned against UN-authorised interventions.",
 now="Libya remains divided between rival governments, and the intervention's outcome made Russia and China wary of approving UN action to protect civilians.",
 story="Protests in February 2011 became an armed revolt. The UN authorised a no-fly zone to protect civilians in March, and NATO air strikes helped the rebels take Tripoli in August. Libyan leader Muammar Gaddafi was captured and killed near Sirte on 20 October.",
 why="Gaddafi's violent crackdown on protesters led the UN to authorise a no-fly zone to protect civilians.",
 then="Libya split between rival governments in Tripoli and the east and dozens of militias, and became a hub for arms and for migration to Europe.",
 fact="Russia and China abstained on the UN resolution of March 2011 and later cited its use to block action on Syria."),
"d-mubarak": dict(
 bluf="Egyptian protesters set out to end Hosni Mubarak's 30-year rule, and the army set out to survive his fall. Eighteen days of mass protest from 25 January 2011 ended when the army pushed Mubarak out. The uprising showed the power of a crowd and the army's role as kingmaker, and Egypt's democratic experiment ended in a military coup in 2013.",
 now="Egyptian President Abdel Fattah el-Sisi has ruled since 2014 with tighter repression than Mubarak's, a common end for the Arab Spring revolts.",
 story="Protests began in Cairo's Tahrir Square on 25 January 2011. Attacks by police and hired thugs failed to disperse the crowds, which grew. On 11 February the army pushed Egyptian President Hosni Mubarak out.",
 why="Repression, corruption and youth unemployment came first. Tunisia supplied the spark.",
 then="The Muslim Brotherhood's Mohamed Morsi was elected president in 2012 and overthrown by the army in 2013.",
 fact="Mubarak was tried in a cage in a Cairo courtroom, sentenced to life in 2012 and cleared on retrial in 2017."),
"as201477": dict(
 bluf="ISIS leader Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi set out to hold territory and rule it as a caliphate. In 2014 his fighters took Mosul and declared one across eastern Syria and western Iraq, enslaved and massacred Yazidis and inspired attacks worldwide. A US-led coalition and local forces destroyed the territorial caliphate by 2019, and its offshoots remain active in Africa and Asia.",
 now="ISIS offshoots remain active, especially in Africa's Sahel and in Afghanistan, and thousands of its fighters and their families are still held in camps in Syria.",
 story="Syria's civil war and Iraq's sectarian exclusion of Sunnis gave ISIS space and recruits. In June 2014 ISIS fighters captured Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city. ISIS leader Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi declared a 'caliphate' across eastern Syria and western Iraq. ISIS massacred and enslaved Yazidis and staged killings for propaganda.",
 why="Syria's war left ungoverned space. Iraq's exclusion of Sunnis gave ISIS recruits.",
 then="A US-led coalition with Iraqi and Kurdish forces destroyed the caliphate by 2019, after battles that ruined Mosul and Raqqa.",
 fact="Al-Baghdadi died in a US raid in northwest Syria in October 2019."),
"me-jcpoa": dict(
 bluf="The United States and five other powers wanted Iran's nuclear programme capped under inspection; Iran wanted sanctions relief. The deal of 2015 did both for three years. The US withdrawal in 2018 unravelled it, Iran expanded enrichment, and in 2025 Israel and the United States struck Iran's nuclear sites.",
 now="Iran's nuclear programme remains one of the region's most dangerous disputes, and the strikes of 2025 left its future unclear.",
 story="After two years of talks, Iran and the United States, Britain, France, Germany, Russia and China signed the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action in Vienna in July 2015. It capped Iran's uranium enrichment and stockpile under international inspection in exchange for relief from nuclear-related sanctions.",
 why="Western governments feared Iran was moving toward a nuclear weapon. Iran needed sanctions relief.",
 then="US President Donald Trump withdrew in 2018 and reimposed sanctions. Iran then expanded enrichment. In June 2025 Israel and then the United States struck Iran's nuclear sites.",
 fact="The deal cut Iran's enriched uranium stockpile by about 98 per cent, by the White House's account of 2015."),
"me-abraham": dict(
 bluf="Israel and the United Arab Emirates and Bahrain, with the Trump administration brokering, set out to normalise relations over shared concern about Iran and the appeal of trade, without waiting for a Palestinian state. The accords of 2020 were the first Arab normalisations with Israel since Jordan in 1994, and Morocco and Sudan followed. They survived the Gaza war, and Saudi Arabia says its own normalisation depends on a path to a Palestinian state.",
 now="The accords have survived the Gaza war, though relations cooled, and Saudi Arabia says normalisation depends on a path to a Palestinian state.",
 story="US President Donald Trump's administration brokered the accords, signed at the White House in September 2020 by Israel, the United Arab Emirates and Bahrain. They were the first Arab normalisations with Israel since Jordan in 1994 and did not depend on a Palestinian settlement. Morocco and Sudan joined later in 2020.",
 why="Shared concern about Iran came first. Trade and technology ties, and US inducements to each signatory, followed.",
 then="Talks on Saudi-Israeli normalisation were under way in 2023 when the 7 October attack froze them.",
 fact="Morocco joined after the United States recognised its claim to Western Sahara."),
"as202379": dict(
 bluf="Hamas set out to break the Gaza blockade and Israel's hold on the territories by force, and, on one reading, to derail Saudi-Israeli normalisation. On 7 October 2023 its fighters broke through the border fence and killed about 1,200 people in Israel and took 251 hostages, by Israeli counts, the deadliest day in Israel's history. Israel declared war the next day, and the war that followed drew in Hezbollah, the Houthis and Iran and remade Israeli politics and the region's alignments.",
 now="The attack remade Israeli politics and the region's alignments; a ceasefire in October 2025 freed the last living hostages, and Gaza's future remains unresolved.",
 story="On 7 October 2023 Hamas-led fighters broke through the Gaza border fence and attacked Israeli towns, army posts and a music festival. About 1,200 people were killed and 251 taken hostage into Gaza, by Israeli government counts. It was the deadliest day in Israel's history.",
 why="Hamas, which rejects Israel's existence, called the attack a response to occupation, blockade and events at the al-Aqsa mosque. One reading holds that it also aimed to derail the Saudi-Israeli normalisation talks then under way; the other holds that the aim was to restore the Palestinian question to the centre of regional politics.",
 then="Israel declared war on Hamas on 8 October 2023, beginning the longest and deadliest war in the conflict's history.",
 fact="The attack came 50 years and a day after the surprise attack of 6 October 1973."),
"as202379b": dict(
 bluf="Israel set out to destroy Hamas and free the hostages taken on 7 October 2023; Hamas set out to survive and to trade hostages for prisoners and a ceasefire. Two years of air and ground war killed tens of thousands of Palestinians by the Gaza health ministry's count, displaced most of Gaza's 2.1 million people and left the territory in ruins. The war spread to Lebanon, Yemen and Iran, produced cases at international courts, and ended in a US-brokered ceasefire in October 2025 that freed the last living hostages.",
 now="The war's toll and the humanitarian crisis reshaped Middle East politics and led to cases at the International Court of Justice and the International Criminal Court.",
 story="Israel's air and ground campaign from October 2023 aimed to destroy Hamas and free the hostages. Most of Gaza's 2.1 million people were displaced, and the Gaza health ministry reported tens of thousands of Palestinians killed, over 60,000 by its count of 2025; Israel disputes the figures and says a large share were fighters. Hostages were freed in exchanges for Palestinian prisoners during ceasefires in November 2023, early 2025 and October 2025.",
 why="Israel's government held that only Hamas's destruction would prevent another 7 October. Hamas held hostages as its only leverage for a ceasefire and prisoner releases.",
 then="The fighting spread to Lebanon and Yemen and led to direct exchanges of strikes between Israel and Iran. South Africa brought a genocide case against Israel at the International Court of Justice, which Israel rejects, and the International Criminal Court issued arrest warrants for Israeli and Hamas leaders in 2024.",
 fact="The October 2025 ceasefire followed a 20-point plan announced by US President Donald Trump."),
}
